      </solution>
    </example>

    <p>Since there were <em>two</em> variables in the above <xref ref="solnsets-eg-line"/>, the solution set is a subset of <m>\R^2</m>.  Since <em>one</em> of the variables was free, the solution set is a <em>line</em>:
        </p>
<image width="60%">
<shortdescription>
<!-- FILL: Brief alt-text for this TikZ figure (linear algebra context) -->
</shortdescription>
<description>
<p>
<!-- FILL: Complete accessibility description of this TikZ figure for a linear algebra course -->
</p>
</description>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}
  \path[clip] (-6,-2) rectangle (6,2);
  \draw[grid lines] (-6,-2) grid (6,2);
  \draw[thick] (-6,0) -- (6,0);
  \draw[thick] (0,-2) -- (0,2);
  \draw[seq4] (-6,-2) -- node[pos=.4, above left=1pt, whitebg, thin border]
   {$Ax=0$} (6,2);
  \draw[vector, seq-green] (0,0) -- (3,1);
\end{tikzpicture}
              ]]></latex-image>
</image>




    <assemblage>
      <idx><h>System of linear equations</h><h>nontrivial solution</h><h>finding</h></idx>
      <p>In order to actually <em>find</em> a nontrivial solution to <m>Ax=0</m> in the above <xref ref="solnsets-eg-line"/>, it suffices to substitute any nonzero value for the free variable <m>x_2</m>.  For instance, taking <m>x_2=1</m> gives the nontrivial solution <m>x = 1\cdot{3\choose 1} = {3\choose 1}.</m>  Compare to <xref ref="finding-actual-soln"/>.</p>
    </assemblage>

    <example xml:id="solnsets-eg-plane">
      <title>The solution set is a plane</title>
      <statement>
        <p>Compute the parametric vector form of the solution set of <m>Ax=0</m>, where
        <md>
<mrow>A = \begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp -1 \amp 2 \\
-2 \amp 2 \amp -4
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>We row reduce (without augmenting, as suggested in the above <xref ref="solnsets-no-augment"/>):
        <md>
<mrow>\begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp -1 \amp 2 \\
-2 \amp 2 \amp -4
\end{pmatrix} \quad\xrightarrow{\text{RREF}}\quad
        \begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp -1 \amp 2 \\
0 \amp 0 \amp 0
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
        This corresponds to the single equation <m>x_1 - x_2 + 2x_3 = 0</m>.  We  write the parametric form including the redundant equations <m>x_2=x_2</m> and <m>x_3=x_3</m>:
        <md>
<mrow>\left\{\begin{alignedat}{2}
x_1 \amp {}={} \amp x_2 - 2x_3 \amp {} \\
x_2 \amp {}={} \amp x_2 \amp {} \\
x_3 \amp {}={} \amp x_3 \amp .
\end{alignedat}\right.</mrow>
</md>
        We turn these into a single vector equation:
        <md>
<mrow>x = \begin{pmatrix}
x_1 \\
x_2 \\
x_3
\end{pmatrix} = x_2\begin{pmatrix}
1 \\
1 \\
0
\end{pmatrix} + x_3\begin{pmatrix}
-2 \\
0 \\
1
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
        This is the parametric vector form of the solution set.
        Since <m>x_2</m> and <m>x_3</m> are allowed to be anything, this says that the solution set is the set of all linear combinations of <m>\begin{pmatrix}
1 \\
1 \\
0
\end{pmatrix}</m> and <m>\begin{pmatrix}
-2 \\
0 \\
1
\end{pmatrix}</m>.  In other words, the solution set is
        <md>
<mrow>\Span\left\{\begin{pmatrix}
1 \\
1 \\
0
\end{pmatrix},\;\begin{pmatrix}
-2 \\
0 \\
1
\end{pmatrix}\right\}</mrow>
</md>.
        We know how to draw the span of two noncollinear vectors in <m>\R^3</m>: it is a plane.  Therefore, this is a picture of the solution set:
        </p>
<image width="60%">
<shortdescription>
<!-- FILL: Brief alt-text for this TikZ figure (linear algebra context) -->
</shortdescription>
<description>
<p>
<!-- FILL: Complete accessibility description of this TikZ figure for a linear algebra course -->
</p>
</description>
