<exercises>
  <exercise>
    <statement correct="yes">
      <p>If <m>A</m> is a <m>4 \times 6</m> matrix with four pivots, then
        the matrix equation <m>Ax = b</m> must be consistent for every <m>b</m> in
        <m>{\bf R}^4</m>.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>True.  Since <m>A</m> has <m>4</m> pivots, it follows that for
          any vector <m>b</m> in <m>{\bf R}^4</m>, the matrix <m>(A \ | \ b)</m> will have
          a pivot in each of its <m>4</m> rows, and each pivot will be to the
          left of the augment bar.  Therefore, the system <m>Ax = b</m> will be
          consistent.</p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>If <m>A</m> is a <m>3 \times 5</m> matrix with three pivots, then
        the set of solutions to <m>Ax = 0</m> is a plane.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>True.  Since <m>A</m> has <m>3</m> pivots but <m>5</m> columns, there
          will be exactly <m>2</m> columns that do not have a pivot.  Therefore,
          the system <m>Ax = 0</m> will have exactly two free variables, which
          means that its solution set is a plane.</p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement correct="yes">
      <p>If <m>b</m> is a nonzero vector and <m>Ax=b</m> has infinitely many
      solutions, then <m>Ax=0</m> must have infinitely many solutions.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>True.  The solution set to <m>Ax=b</m> is a translate of the solution set to
      <m>Ax=0</m>, hence <m>Ax=0</m> must also have infinitely many solutions.</p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement correct="no">
      <p>If <m>A</m> is a <m>3 \times 4</m> matrix and the solution set for
      <m>Ax=0</m> is a line, then <m>A</m> has exactly <m>2</m> pivots.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>False.  The solution set to <m>Ax=0</m> will be a line if and only if <m>A</m> has
      exactly <m>1</m> free variable.  Since <m>A</m> has <m>4</m> columns, this means <m>A</m> has
      exactly <m>3</m> (not <m>2</m>) pivots.</p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement correct="no">
      <p>There is a <m>3 \times 2</m> matrix <m>A</m> whose homogeneous solution set
      <m>Ax=0</m> is the line <m>x_1 + x_2 = 1</m>.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>False.  The zero vector <m>x_1 = x_2 = 0</m> does not satisfy the equation
      <m>x_1 + x_2 = 1</m>.</p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>Suppose that the set of solutions to <m>Ax = b</m> is the plane <m>z=1</m>.  What is the
      set of solutions to <m>Ax = 0</m>?</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>From the theory of section 2.4, the solution set to <m>Ax = 0</m> is the plane
        through the origin parallel to the plane <m>z=1</m>.  Therefore, the solution set
        to <m>Ax = 0</m> is the <m>x_1x_2</m>-plane in <m>{\bf R}^3</m> (this is the plane <m>z=0</m>).</p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>Suppose that the set of solutions to <m>Ax = 0</m> is the plane
      <m>y=x</m> in <m>{\bf R}^2</m>.  Is it possible that there is a <m>b</m> so that
      the set of solutions to <m>Ax = b</m> is the line <m>x+y=1</m>?  Justify your answer.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>No.  If <m>Ax=b</m> is consistent, then its solution set must be parallel to the solution set to <m>Ax=0</m>, namely the line <m>y=x</m> which has slope <m>1</m>.  The line <m>x+y=1</m> is the line <m>y= -x + 1</m>, which has slope <m>-1</m>, so it cannot be the solution set to any consistent system <m>Ax=b</m>.</p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>Suppose that the set of solutions to <m>Ax = 0</m> is the plane
      <m>x+y = 0</m> in <m>{\bf R}^3</m>.  Is it possible that there is a <m>b</m> so that
      the set of solutions to <m>Ax = b</m> is the <m>z</m>-axis in <m>{\bf R}^3</m>?  Justify your answer.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>No.  The solution set to <m>Ax=0</m> is a plane, then the solution set of any
        consistent equation <m>Ax=b</m> must be a parallel plane, but the <m>z</m>-axis is a
        line.</p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>Suppose that the set of solutions to <m>Ax = 0</m> is the plane
      <m>x + 2y - 3z = 0</m> in <m>{\bf R}^3</m> and that the vector <m>\begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 3 \\ 5\end{pmatrix}</m> is a solution to <m>Ax = b</m>.</p>
    </introduction>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>Find one other solution to <m>Ax=b</m>.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          We can get the solution set to <m>Ax=b</m> by taking any particular solution to <m>Ax = b</m> (in this case, <m>\begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 3 \\ 5\end{pmatrix}</m>) and then adding all homogeneous solutions.  The solution set to <m>Ax=0</m> is the plane <m>x+2y-3z=0</m>, and we can find a homogeneous solution by just guessing and checking to find <m>x</m>, <m>y</m>, and <m>z</m> that satisfy the equation.  One such homogeneous solution is <m>\begin{pmatrix}3 \\ 0 \\ 1\end{pmatrix}</m>.  Another solution to <m>Ax = b</m> is
          <md><mrow>\begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 3 \\ 5\end{pmatrix} + \begin{pmatrix}3 \\ 0 \\ 1\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}4 \\ 3 \\ 6\end{pmatrix}</mrow></md>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>Find all solutions to <m>Ax=b</m>.  Write your answer in parametric vector form.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The homogeneous equation <m>x+2y-3z=0</m> corresponds to <m>\left(\begin{array}{rrr|r}1 \amp 2 \amp -3 \amp 0\end{array}\right)</m>, which gives <m>x = -2y + 3z</m> where <m>y</m> and <m>z</m> are free.
          <md><mrow>\begin{pmatrix}x \\ y \\ z\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}-2y+3z \\ y \\ z\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}-2y \\ y \\ 0\end{pmatrix} + \begin{pmatrix}3z \\ 0 \\ z\end{pmatrix}
          = y \begin{pmatrix}-2 \\ 1 \\ 0\end{pmatrix} + z \begin{pmatrix}3 \\ 0 \\ 1\end{pmatrix}</mrow></md>.
          The solution set to <m>Ax = b</m> is the translation of the homogeneous solution set by the particular solution <m>\begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 3 \\ 5\end{pmatrix}</m>:
          <md><mrow>\begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 3 \\ 5\end{pmatrix} + y \begin{pmatrix}-2 \\ 1 \\ 0\end{pmatrix} + z \begin{pmatrix}3 \\ 0 \\ 1\end{pmatrix}, \quad \textup{ where } y, z \textup{ real}</mrow></md>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>Is there a <m>2 \times 2</m> matrix so that the set of solutions to <m>Av = \begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 2\end{pmatrix}</m> is the line <m>y = x + 1</m>?  If so, find such an <m>A</m>.  If not, explain why not.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        Yes: we need <m>\begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 2\end{pmatrix}</m> to be in the span of the columns of <m>A</m>, so that <m>Av = \begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 2\end{pmatrix}</m> is consistent.  Also, we need for the line <m>y=x</m> to be the set of solutions to <m>Ax=0</m> since that is the line through the origin parallel to <m>y=x+1</m>.  One way to get <m>A</m> is to start as if both columns are <m>\begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 2\end{pmatrix}</m>, then modify <m>A</m> until we satisfy the above.  One such matrix is
        <md><mrow>A = \begin{pmatrix}-1 \amp 1 \\ -2 \amp 2\end{pmatrix}</mrow></md>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>Fill in the blanks: If <m>A</m> is a <m>5 \times 3</m> matrix with two pivots,
      then the set of solutions to <m>Ax = 0</m> is a \underline{\qquad \quad}
      that lives in \underline{\qquad}.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p><m>A</m> has two pivot columns, so we will get one free variable in the
        when we solve <m>Ax = 0</m>, therefore the solution set will be a line.
        The solution set lives in <m>{\bf R}^3</m> since <m>A</m> has three columns.</p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Let
        <md><mrow>A = \begin{pmatrix}1 \amp -2 \amp 1 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 2\end{pmatrix}, \qquad
          b_1 = \begin{pmatrix}1 \\ -1 \\ 0\end{pmatrix}, \qquad
          b_2 = \begin{pmatrix}2 \\ 3\end{pmatrix}</mrow></md>.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>Which matrix equation makes sense: <m>Ax = b_1</m> or
          <m>Ax = b_2</m>?</p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p><m>A</m> is a <m>2 \times 3</m> matrix, so any solution <m>x</m> must
            be in <m>{\bf R}^3</m> and the target vector must be in <m>{\bf R}^2</m>.
            Therefore, <m>Ax = b_2</m> makes sense but <m>Ax = b_1</m> does not. One way
            to check this is to translate the matrix equation into a system of
            equations to confirm our answer, which we do in part (b).</p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>For the answer you chose in part (a), write the matrix
          equation as a system of equations.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The linear system corresponding to <m>Ax = b_2</m> is
          <md><mrow>x_1 - 2x_2 + x_3 = 2</mrow></md>
          <md><mrow>0x_1 + x_2 + 2x_3 = 3</mrow></md>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>For the answer you chose in part (a), write the matrix
          equation as a vector equation.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The corresponding vector equation is
          <md><mrow>x_1 \begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 0\end{pmatrix} + x_2 \begin{pmatrix}-2 \\ 1\end{pmatrix} + x_3 \begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 2\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}2 \\ 3\end{pmatrix}</mrow></md>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>Solve the matrix equation and write your answer in
          parametric vector form.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          We row-reduce <m>(A \ | \ b_2)</m>:
          <md><mrow>\left(\begin{array}{rrr|r}1 \amp -2 \amp 1 \amp 2 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 2 \amp 3\end{array}\right) \xrightarrow[]{R_1 = R_1 + 2R_2}
                \left(\begin{array}{rrr|r}1 \amp 0 \amp 5 \amp 8 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 2 \amp 3\end{array}\right)</mrow></md>.
          This gives us <m>x_1 + 5 x_3 = 8</m> and
              <m>x_2 + 2x_3 = 3</m>, with <m>x_3</m> free.
          <md><mrow>x_1 = 8 - 5x_3, \qquad x_2 = 3 - 2x_3, \qquad x_3 = x_3
                \textup{ (free)}</mrow></md>.
          We finish the problem by putting this into parametric vector form:
          <md><mrow>\begin{pmatrix}x_1 \\ x_2 \\ x_3\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}8-5x_3 \\ 3-2x_3 \\ x_3\end{pmatrix}
                \ = \ \begin{pmatrix}8 \\ 3 \\ 0\end{pmatrix} + \begin{pmatrix}-5x_3 \\ -2x_3 \\ x_3\end{pmatrix}
                \ = \ \begin{pmatrix}8 \\ 3 \\ 0\end{pmatrix} + x_3 \begin{pmatrix}-5 \\ -2 \\ 1\end{pmatrix}</mrow></md>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Consider the system of equations in <m>x_1</m>, <m>x_2</m>, <m>x_3</m>, and <m>x_4</m>
          given below.
        <md><mrow>x_1 - x_2 + 2x_3 - 2x_4 \ = \ -1</mrow></md>
        <md><mrow>-x_1 + x_2 -2x_3 + x_4 = \ 2</mrow></md>
        <md><mrow>-4x_1 + 4x_2 -8x_3 + 6x_4 \ = \ 6</mrow></md>.
        Put an augmented matrix
            into reduced row echelon form to solve the system of equations.
            Write your answer in parametric vector form.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        <md>
          <mrow>\left(\begin{array}{rrrr|r}1 \amp -1 \amp 2 \amp -2 \amp -1</mrow>
          <mrow>-1 \amp 1 \amp -2 \amp 1 \amp 2</mrow>
          <mrow>-4 \amp 4 \amp -8 \amp 6 \amp 6\end{array}\right)
                \amp
                  \xrightarrow[R_3=R_3+4R_1]{R_2=R_2+R_1}
                  \left(\begin{array}{rrrr|r}1 \amp -1 \amp 2 \amp -2 \amp -1</mrow>
          <mrow>0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp -1 \amp 1</mrow>
          <mrow>0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp -2 \amp 2\end{array}\right)
                  \xrightarrow[R_3= R_3/-2]{R_2= \ -R_2}
                  \left(\begin{array}{rrrr|r}1 \amp -1 \amp 2 \amp -2 \amp -1</mrow>
          <mrow>0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp -1</mrow>
          <mrow>0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp -1\end{array}\right)</mrow>
          <mrow></mrow>
          <mrow>\amp  \xrightarrow[R_1= R_1+2R_2]{R_3=R_3-R_2}
                  \left(\begin{array}{rrrr|r}1 \amp -1 \amp 2 \amp 0 \amp -3</mrow>
          <mrow>0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp -1</mrow>
          <mrow>0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0\end{array}\right)</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        The free variables are <m>x_2</m> and <m>x_3</m>:
        <md><mrow>x_1= x_2-2x_3-3, \qquad x_2=x_2, \qquad x_3=x_3, \qquad x_4=
                -1</mrow></md>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <md><mrow>\begin{pmatrix}x_1 \\ x_2 \\ x_3 \\ x_4\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}x_2-2x_3-3 \\ x_2 \\ x_3 \\ -1\end{pmatrix}
                = \begin{pmatrix}x_2 \\ x_2 \\ 0 \\ 0\end{pmatrix} + \begin{pmatrix}-2x_3 \\ 0 \\ x_3 \\ 0\end{pmatrix} + \begin{pmatrix}-3 \\ 0 \\ 0 \\ -1\end{pmatrix} \
                = \ \boxed{\begin{pmatrix}-3 \\ 0 \\ 0 \\ -1\end{pmatrix} + x_2 \begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 1 \\ 0 \\ 0\end{pmatrix} + x_3 \begin{pmatrix}-2 \\ 0 \\ 1 \\ 0\end{pmatrix}}</mrow></md>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Consider the linear system of equations in <m>x_1</m>, <m>x_2</m>,
        <m>x_3</m>, and <m>x_4</m> given below.
        <md><mrow>-2x_1 - 2x_2 + x_3 - 4x_4 = -8</mrow></md>
        <md><mrow>-x_1 - x_2 + x_3 - 2x_4 = -5</mrow></md>
        <md><mrow>4x_1 + 4x_2 - x_3 + 8x_4 = 14</mrow></md>.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>Write the set of solutions to the system in parametric
          vector form.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          We put the system into an augmented matrix and
            row-reduce:
          <md><mrow>\left(\begin{array}{rrrr|r}-2 \amp -2 \amp 1 \amp -4 \amp -8 \\ -1 \amp -1 \amp 1 \amp -2 \amp -5 \\ 4 \amp 4 \amp -1 \amp 8 \amp 14\end{array}\right)
              \xrightarrow[]{RREF}
              \left(\begin{array}{rrrr|r}1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 2 \amp 3 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp -2 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0\end{array}\right)</mrow></md>.
          Therefore, <m>x_1 = 3 - x_2 - 2x_4</m> and <m>x_3 = -2</m> while <m>x_2</m> and
            <m>x_4</m> are free:
          <md><mrow>\begin{pmatrix}x_1 \\ x_2 \\ x_3 \\ x_4\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}3-x_2-2x_4 \\ x_2 \\ -2 \\ x_4\end{pmatrix}
              = \boxed{\begin{pmatrix}3 \\ 0 \\ -2 \\ 0\end{pmatrix} + x_2 \begin{pmatrix}-1 \\ 1 \\ 0 \\ 0\end{pmatrix} + x_4 \begin{pmatrix}-2 \\ 0 \\ 0 \\ 1\end{pmatrix}}</mrow></md>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>Write two different solutions to the system of equations.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          We can obtain a solution to the system by choosing any
            values we wish for <m>x_2</m> and <m>x_4</m>.  For example, if we choose <m>x_2 = 0</m>
            and <m>x_4 = 0</m> we get
          <md><mrow>\begin{pmatrix}x_1 \\ x_2 \\ x_3 \\ x_4\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}3 \\ 0 \\ -2 \\ 0\end{pmatrix}</mrow></md>.
          If we choose <m>x_2 = 1</m> and <m>x_4 = 0</m> we get
          <md><mrow>\begin{pmatrix}x_1 \\ x_2 \\ x_3 \\ x_4\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}2 \\ 1 \\ -2 \\ 0\end{pmatrix}</mrow></md>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>Write two <em>nonzero</em> solutions to the corresponding
          <em>homogeneous</em> linear system of equations.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>The solutions to the corresponding homogeneous system
          (where <m>-8</m>, <m>-5</m>, and <m>14</m> are all replaced by zero) are given by
          everything in the span of the two vectors <m>\begin{pmatrix}-1 \\ 1 \\ 0 \\ 0\end{pmatrix}</m> and
          <m>\begin{pmatrix}-2 \\ 0 \\ 0 \\ 1\end{pmatrix}</m>.  Therefore, <m>\begin{pmatrix}-1 \\ 1 \\ 0 \\ 0\end{pmatrix}</m> and <m>\begin{pmatrix}-2 \\ 0 \\ 0 \\ 1\end{pmatrix}</m>
          are two solutions to the homogeneous equations.</p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>Suppose <m>A</m> is a <m>2 \times 2</m> matrix and that for some vector <m>b</m>, the
      set of solutions to <m>Ax = b</m> is the line <m>x_2 = 4-x_1</m>.  Write the
      general solution to the homogeneous equation <m>Ax=0</m> in parametric
      vector form.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        The solution set for <m>Ax = 0</m> must include the origin and also must
          be a translate to the solution set to <m>Ax = b</m>.  In other words,
          the solution set for <m>Ax = 0</m> is the line through the origin that
          is parallel to <m>x_2 = 4-x_1</m>.  This is the line <m>x_2 = -x_1</m>,
          so <m>x_1 = -x_2</m> and <m>x_2</m> is free.
        <md><mrow>\begin{pmatrix}x_1 \\ x_2\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}-x_2 \\ x_2\end{pmatrix} = x_2 \begin{pmatrix}-1 \\ 1\end{pmatrix}</mrow></md>.
        Another way to do this problem is to draw the line <m>x_2 = 4-x_1</m>,
          then draw its parallel line through the origin and notice that this
          line is the span of <m>\begin{pmatrix}-1 \\ 1\end{pmatrix}</m> (or equivalently the span of
          <m>\begin{pmatrix}1 \\ -1\end{pmatrix}</m>).
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>For some matrix <m>A</m> and some vector <m>b</m>, the dashed line below
      is the solution set to the matrix equation <m>Ax = b</m>.</p>
      <image source="images/2_4/solution_sets1.pdf">
        <shortdescription><!-- FILL: concise image description --></shortdescription>
        <description><p><!-- FILL: complete image description --></p></description>
      </image>
    </introduction>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>On the same graph, draw the solution set to <m>Ax = 0</m>.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>The solution set is the line through the origin that
            is parallel to the dashed blue line.</p>
        <image source="images/2_4/solution_sets1-sol.pdf">
          <shortdescription><!-- FILL: concise image description --></shortdescription>
          <description><p><!-- FILL: complete image description --></p></description>
        </image>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>True or false: It is possible that for some vector <m>v</m>,
          the solution set to <m>Ax = v</m> is a single point.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>False.  If <m>Ax = v</m> is consistent, then its solution
            set must be a translation of the solution set to <m>Ax = 0</m>, which
            is a line.  If <m>Ax = v</m> is inconsistent, then it has no solution.
            Either way, it is not possible for <m>Ax = v</m> to have exactly one
            solution.</p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Write a system of linear equations whose solution set has parametric
        form
        <md><mrow>x = \begin{pmatrix}3 \\ 0\end{pmatrix} + x_2 \begin{pmatrix}2 \\ 1\end{pmatrix}</mrow></md>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        Writing <m>x</m> as a single vector, we get:
        <md><mrow>x = \begin{pmatrix}3 \\ 0\end{pmatrix} + \begin{pmatrix}2x_2 \\ x_2\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}3+2x_2 \\ x_2\end{pmatrix}</mrow></md>.
        Therefore,
          <m>x_1 = 3+x_2</m> and <m>x_2 = x_2</m> (free).  One possible answer is the
          following single equation, which will give us <m>x_1 = 3+x_2</m> with <m>x_2</m>
          as a free variable:
        <md><mrow>x_1 - x_2 = 3</mrow></md>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>The diagram below describes traffic in a part of town.</p>
      <image>
        <shortdescription><!-- TODO: concise alt text for this TikZ image --></shortdescription>
        <description>
          <p><!-- TODO: complete description of this TikZ image --></p>
        </description>
        <latex-image>
          \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.4, thick,
                every node/.style={inner sep=3pt, label distance=1mm}]
              \node at (0,2.4) {Traffic flow (cars/hr)};
              \point[scale=1.5] (A) at (0,1);

              \point[scale=1.5] (C) at (1,-1);
              \point[scale=1.5] (D) at (-1,-1);

              \draw[mid arrow] (A.center) to["$x_1$"] (C.center);
              \draw[mid arrow] (D.center) to["$x_2$"] (C.center);
              \draw[mid arrow] (D.center) to["$x_3$"] (A.center);
              \draw[rmid arrow=.3] (A.center) to["$110$"] +(-1/2,1);
              \draw[mid arrow=.5] (A.center) to["$40$" swap] +(1/2,1);



              \draw[mid arrow=.7] (C.center) to["$320$"] +(1,0);
              \draw[mid arrow=.7] (C.center) to["$40$"] +(0,-1);
              \draw[rmid arrow=.7] (D.center) to["$200$" swap] +(-1,0);
              \draw[rmid arrow=.3] (D.center) to["$90$" swap] +(0,-1);
            \end{tikzpicture}
        </latex-image>
      </image>
    </introduction>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>Write a system of three linear equations in <m>x_1</m>, <m>x_2</m>, and
          <m>x_3</m> corresponding to the traffic flow.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          For the top, bottom right, and bottom left nodes, the
             number of cars entering must match the number of cars exiting, so
             the system is:
          <md><mrow>x_1 + 40 = x_3 + 110</mrow></md>
          <md><mrow>x_1 + x_2 = 360</mrow></md>
          <md><mrow>x_2 + x_3 = 290</mrow></md>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>Use an augmented matrix to solve this system of linear equations.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The system can be written
          <md><mrow>x_1 - x_3 = 70</mrow></md>
          <md><mrow>x_1 + x_2 = 360</mrow></md>
          <md><mrow>x_2 + x_3 = 290</mrow></md>.
          We form an augmented matrix and perform row
             operations.
          <md><mrow>\left(\begin{array}{rrr|r}1 \amp 0 \amp -1 \amp 70 \\ 1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 360 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 1 \amp 290\end{array}\right)
               \xrightarrow[]{R_2 = R_2 - R_1} \left(\begin{array}{rrr|r}1 \amp 0 \amp -1 \amp 70 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 1 \amp 290 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 1 \amp 290\end{array}\right) \xrightarrow[]{R_3 = R_3 - R_2} \left(\begin{array}{rrr|r}1 \amp 0 \amp -1 \amp 70 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 1 \amp 290 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0\end{array}\right)</mrow></md>.
          Therefore, <m>x_3</m> is a free variable,
             <m>x_1 = x_3 + 70</m>, and <m>x_2 = 290-x_3</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>Were we given enough information to know the exact
          values of <m>x_1</m>, <m>x_2</m>, and <m>x_3</m>?  If your answer is yes, justify why.
          If your answer is no, give the range of possible values for <m>x_1</m>,
          <m>x_2</m>, and <m>x_3</m>.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>We cannot know the exact values of <m>x_1</m>, <m>x_2</m>, and
           <m>x_3</m> with the information we have only been given.  For example,
           we could have <m>x_3=0</m>, <m>x_2 = 290</m>, <m>x_1=70</m>.  Or, we could have
           <m>x_3= 100</m>, <m>x_2= 190</m>, <m>x_1=170</m>, etc.</p>
        <p>Even though <m>x_3</m> is a free variable in the system of equations,
           the real-world constraints of the problem make us consider what is
           actually possible here.  Since the variables are counting the
           number of cars at each intersection, they must all be at least
           zero.  Therefore, we need to have <m>x_3 \geq 0</m> (whereby
           <m>x_1 = x_3 + 70</m>), and since <m>x_2 = 290-x_3 \geq 0</m> we also need
           <m>x_3 \leq 290</m>.</p>
        <p>To summarize: As the values of <m>x_3</m> range from <m>0</m> to <m>290</m>, the
           values of <m>x_1</m> range from <m>70</m> to <m>360</m> while the values for <m>x_3</m>
           range from <m>290</m> (when <m>x_3=0</m>) down to <m>0</m> (when <m>x_3 = 290</m>).</p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>
</exercises> 
</section>
